  "rationale": "Twelve baseline responses hit 1,020 tokens, consuming 12,240 of 13,941 output tokens; nine fail strict grading. I hypothesize that persistent repetition displaces constraint completion. Four source-CPU contrasts pair actual looping baseline prefixes with editor-written task-completing references. Layer 19 has the largest four-pair-consistent positive writer mass (0.601); layer 22 supplies 0.368. Select all 63 eligible layer-19 columns and the top 64 eligible layer-22 columns, requiring positive attribution in every pair. Strength 2 reverses each selected writer\u2019s failure-direction component; it corresponds to approximately 29% and 16% of the respective observed residual contrasts under a fixed-activation approximation. This dose aims to interrupt loops without a maximal intervention.\n\nPrediction: more completed formatting/count constraints and fewer generated tokens, lowering total response latency and tokens per strict pass. No substantial fixed-memory reduction is predicted. Premature termination could instead break minimum-length/count requirements or remove required keywords. References are hypotheses, not verified model successes; unequal continuation lengths and content confound the direction. Source BF16 CPU activations differ from deployed q4 WebGPU execution. Four pairs and one exposed 32-item development comparison cannot establish a repetition-specific mechanism, generalization, or robust resource causality.",
